  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA crossing weak shortwave trough, and convergence along a lake\nbreeze boundary, should result in isolated to scattered showers\nand thunderstorms mainly north of Pittsburgh this afternoon and\nearly evening. Capping warmth aloft should preclude more\nsignificant chances for thunderstorms toward PIT and points to\nthe south. Entrainment of dry air aloft should also limit\nupdraft development, with ML CAPE reaching 500-1000 j/kg this\nafternoon north of PIT. If updrafts are able to become\norganized, then gusty wind will be possible with an enhanced\nDCAPE due to the dry air aloft. Otherwise, after some morning\nfog, partly cloudy skies are expected today, with highs near\naverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nUncertainty in the details still exist in the forecast for\nMonday into Tuesday. An MCS is expected to develop tonight\nacross the Upper Midwest, moving southward overnight. This\nactivity is currently expected to stay west of the forecast\narea. Recent CAMs have trended toward less convection on Monday.\nStill expect scattered thunderstorms to develop through the day\nwith building diurnal instability. ML CAPE is progged to range\nfrom 1500-2500 j/kg. Expect scattered convection to develop as\nconvective temperatures are reached. More focused areas of\nthunderstorms will also be possible on any outflow boundaries\nfrom upstream convection. \n\nSome increase in 0-6km shear is expected (30kt), with some dry\nmid level air in place. This should result in a damaging wind\npotential in storms that become organized. Large hail is also\npossible, along with isolated tornadoes as wind increasingly\nveers with height. \n\nRecent CAMS also indicate a potential MCS Monday night into\nTuesday morning, developing over the Great Lakes region and\ntracking SEWD across portions of the Upper Ohio Valley region.\nThis is in advance of an approaching shortwave trough and \nsurface cold front. While instability should wane somewhat \nMonday night, a severe storm potential would still be possible \nwith an organized MCS. Localized flash flooding will be possible\nas well, especially if or where any multiple rounds of storms \noccur. \n\nUncertainty still remains in the exact development, timing, and\ntracks of any MCSs, with a low confidence predictability until \ncloser to the event.\n\nThe trough and surface cold front should cross the Upper Ohio\nValley region on Tuesday, with continued showers and\nthunderstorms. The severe potential will depend on the extent of\nmorning convection and cloud cover, though the latest ML\nguidance begins to shift the best severe potential sewd through\nthe day.",
